      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The immediate aggression you observe stems from the sheer volume of enzyme exposure occurring simultaneously once hundreds of cells breach the protective skin. Unlike peeling, which exposes cells slowly and allows for the gradual diffusion or absorption of precursors, slicing shatters countless compartments instantly, flooding the cutting surface with active alliinase and its substrate. This sudden surge triggers a rapid cascade where precursor molecules are converted into volatile propanethial S-oxide at lightweight speeds, and the gas diffuses outward from the cell wounds over the air interface in mere seconds. Because this volatile molecule is designed to escape the tissue immediately, it overwhelms the olfactory nerves and fills the air around the bowl before students can even brace themselves, creating an aggressive sensory onset that feels like an instant attack rather than a slow build-up.",
      "source": "target",
